  "scope": "Read-only GitGuardian applicability, native receipt coverage and transfer ownership. No integration/protection/token/scan/release mutation.",
  "conclusion": {
    "public": "Current written policy explicitly preserves the existing GitGuardian requirement. Missing current-head check runs/suites are an external integration or evidence gap, not success and not an exemption derived from three visible Actions protection contexts.",
    "private": "The current private AGENTS files do not name a new GitGuardian required status. Hosted UI inherits public security guards. Existing native GitGuardian checks are independently verified in both private repositories; newer Cloud heads lack them. Preserve that existing protection and require its repository/App owner to resolve the gap; do not invent a new status check or transplant an older head's success.",
    "rootCause": "Unknown. Native evidence proves missing check creation on selected newer heads; it does not identify installation, repository selection, event delivery or provider configuration as the cause.",
    "ownerDecision": "The repository/organization and existing GitGuardian App integration owner must verify destination coverage and native event/check delivery, restoring the existing exact-head check where required. The release source App and product App are different integrations; their migration receipts cannot establish GitGuardian readiness."
  },
